# SlideScribe

Turn a recorded conference call into an illustrated PDF transcript: every slide image
sits directly above the words spoken while it was on screen.

[![Open In Colab](https://colab.research.google.com/assets/colab-badge.svg)](https://colab.research.google.com/github/nishitghosh81/slidescribe/blob/main/notebooks/SlideScribe_Colab.ipynb)
[![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-blue.svg)](LICENSE)
[![Python 3.9+](https://img.shields.io/badge/python-3.9+-blue.svg)](https://www.python.org/downloads/)

**Input:** a screen-shared meeting recording.
**Output:** a PDF where the deck and the discussion are back together.

Recordings split the two things you need. The audio has the reasoning; the video has the
diagram someone was pointing at. Scrubbing a 45-minute file to find the moment a chart
appeared is the reason nobody rewatches meetings. SlideScribe puts them back in one
readable document.

---

## Try it without installing anything

Click the Colab badge above, then `Runtime` → `Run all`. Two cells: the first installs,
the second takes your video and hands back a PDF. There is a built-in sample if you want
to see the output before uploading anything of your own.

## Install

```bash
pip install git+https://github.com/nishitghosh81/slidescribe.git
```

`ffmpeg` is not required — video and audio decoding are handled by OpenCV and
faster-whisper's bundled decoder.

## Use it

```bash
# Local only. No API key, no network calls.
slidescribe meeting.mp4

# With a model: smarter slide detection, slide titles, summary and action items.
slidescribe meeting.mp4 --model gpt-4o --api-key sk-...

# Webcam tile on the right? Analyse only the left three quarters of the frame.
slidescribe meeting.mp4 --crop 0,0,0.75,1
```

From Python:

```python
from slidescribe import process_video, Config, LLMConfig

result = process_video("meeting.mp4", "meeting.pdf")
print(result)  # meeting.pdf — 14 slides, 402 segments, 187s

# With a model
config = Config(llm=LLMConfig(model="claude-sonnet-4-5", api_key="sk-ant-..."))
process_video("meeting.mp4", config=config)
```

---

## How it works

```
video ──┬── slide detection ── pHash + SSIM ──┬── [optional] vision model ──┐
        │                                      │   arbitrates ambiguity     │
        │                                                                   ├── PDF
        └── transcription ──── faster-whisper ─── timestamped segments ─────┘
```

**Slide detection** samples one frame per second and compares each against the last using
a perceptual hash (DCT-based) and structural similarity. When a frame differs enough, it
is captured as a new slide.

The interesting part is what *doesn't* count as a change. A cursor moving across a static
slide, a webcam tile updating, or a bullet animating in are all pixel changes that are not
slide changes. Two mechanisms handle this:

- **SSIM must always agree.** Measured on real screen shares, a perceptual hash is
  unstable on flat, high-contrast slides — a cursor crossing a static slide can produce
  the same Hamming distance as a genuine slide change. SSIM separates the two cleanly
  (noise stays above 0.98, real changes drop below 0.96), so pHash acts only as a
  sensitivity boost and never fires alone. This single rule is the difference between 4
  correct captures and 8 duplicates on a typical deck.
- **A minimum gap** between captures, which absorbs animation churn.

**Transcription** uses faster-whisper, a dedicated speech-to-text model. Segments come
back with start and end times.

**Assembly** joins the two by timestamp: each slide owns the transcript segments spoken
between its appearance and the next slide's.

## The LLM is optional, and that is the point

The whole pipeline runs with no API key. Transcription, slide detection and PDF assembly
are local, deterministic, and free. A model is not required for any of it.

What a model adds, when you supply one:

| Feature | Without a model | With a model |
|---|---|---|
| Transcription | faster-whisper | identical — an LLM adds nothing here |
| Slide detection | pHash + SSIM | ambiguous cases arbitrated by vision |
| Slide titles | numbered | titled from slide content |
| Summary and action items | — | generated |
| Transcript cleanup | raw ASR output | punctuated and paragraphed |

The honest split: for a clean recording, local detection is genuinely competitive. Where a
vision model earns its place is messy real-world footage — animated builds, embedded
video, overlapping webcam tiles — and in the post-processing that has no non-LLM
equivalent at all, like naming a slide or extracting action items.

Only frames the CV stage flags as *borderline* are sent to the model, so cost scales with
how ambiguous a recording is rather than how long it is. `max_vision_calls` caps it
regardless.

## Bring your own model

SlideScribe never imports a provider SDK. Model calls go through
[LiteLLM](https://github.com/BerriAI/litellm), so anything it routes to works:

| Provider | `--model` |
|---|---|
| OpenAI | `gpt-4o` |
| Anthropic | `claude-sonnet-4-5` |
| Google | `gemini/gemini-2.0-flash` |
| Groq | `groq/llama-3.3-70b-versatile` |
| OpenRouter | `openrouter/anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5` |
| Ollama (local) | `ollama/llava` with `--api-base http://localhost:11434` |
| vLLM / any OpenAI-compatible | your model name with `--api-base` |

Vision-only features are skipped automatically if the model is text-only — SlideScribe
probes for image support once at startup rather than failing twenty minutes into a run.
A bad key or an unreachable endpoint degrades to no-LLM mode instead of crashing; you
still get a PDF.

Keys are read per-run from the flag or environment (`SLIDESCRIBE_MODEL`,
`SLIDESCRIBE_API_KEY`, `SLIDESCRIBE_API_BASE`) and never written to disk.

---

## Tuning

Defaults are calibrated for 720p/1080p screen shares. The two knobs worth touching:

| Symptom | Fix |
|---|---|
| Duplicate near-identical slides | Raise `--hash-threshold` to 8–10, lower `--ssim-threshold` to 0.92, or raise `--min-slide-seconds` |
| Slides missed | Lower `--hash-threshold` to 4, raise `--ssim-threshold` to 0.97 |
| Webcam tile triggering captures | `--crop 0,0,0.75,1` to analyse only the shared region |
| Poor transcript | `--whisper-model medium` or `large-v3` (GPU recommended), set `--language` |
| Slow on CPU | `--whisper-model base` or `tiny` |

Full option list: `slidescribe --help`.

### Choosing a Whisper model

| Model | Rough speed on a T4 | Use when |
|---|---|---|
| `tiny` / `base` | very fast | CPU-only, or you want a quick draft |
| `small` (default) | fast | the sensible default |
| `medium` / `large-v3` | slow | accented speech, technical vocabulary, GPU available |

## Requirements

Python 3.9+. Core dependencies: `faster-whisper`, `opencv-python-headless`, `numpy`,
`reportlab`, `tqdm`. Add `litellm` for the optional model layer.

A GPU is not required but changes transcription from tens of minutes to a few.

## Development

```bash
git clone https://github.com/nishitghosh81/slidescribe.git
cd slidescribe
pip install -e ".[dev,llm]"
pytest -q
```

The test suite builds synthetic screen recordings containing a moving cursor and a noisy
webcam tile, then asserts that detection finds every real slide change and none of the
fake ones. Both adversarial cases are regression-guarded.

## Limitations

- Tuned for **screen-shared slide decks**. Continuous scrolling, live coding, or
  whiteboarding will over-trigger; raise `--min-slide-seconds` and expect to tune.
- **No speaker labels.** Diarization is a separate model
  ([pyannote.audio](https://github.com/pyannote/pyannote-audio)) and is not wired in yet.
- Long recordings are processed in memory; a multi-hour file on a small machine may need
  a lower `--sample-fps`.
- Slide *images* are captured, not the underlying deck — text in them is not searchable
  unless you enable captioning.

## Roadmap

- Speaker diarization
- HTML and Markdown output alongside PDF
- Embedding-based detection (CLIP/DINOv2) as a middle tier between pHash and a vision model
- Batch mode for multiple recordings

## Contributing

Issues and pull requests welcome. If you hit a recording that detects badly, an issue with
the failure mode (missed slides, duplicates, what the source looked like) is genuinely
useful — detection tuning improves from real examples.

